# resq secrets allowlist
#
# Two categories of false positives are excluded here:
#
# 1. GitHub Actions workflow files (and the generated Agentic-Workflows
#    actions-lock.json) — pinned commit SHAs in `uses:` directives / lock
#    entries (40-char hex strings). SHA-pinned actions are a security best
#    practice, not leaked credentials.
# 2. Per-package CHANGELOG.md files — auto-generated by Changesets. Each
#    release note links to the source PR commit (40-char hex SHA), which the
#    scanner flags as high-entropy hex.
# 3. bun.lock — the lockfile is generated from public npm registry metadata.
#    Its `sha512-<base64>` package-integrity digests are content hashes, not
#    credentials; a large dependency/scope change re-touches hundreds of them.
# 4. Built example bundles under `examples/*/dist/` — minified output, where a
#    run of concatenated identifiers reads as high-entropy hex. Nothing in the
#    example sources carries a credential to bundle in the first place.
# 5. `packages/security/tests/fixtures/corpora.ts` — the attack corpus. It is
#    *supposed* to hold credential-shaped payloads: they are what the
#    `credential_exposure` and `jwt` rules are tested against. Both flagged
#    values are the vendors' own published placeholders —
#    `AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE` from the AWS documentation, and the jwt.io sample
#    token signing `{"sub":"1234567890"}`. Neither authenticates anything.
#
# The scanner only supports literal file-path exclusions, not pattern-based or
# line-level ignores. These entries are scoped to known-safe paths; all other
# files remain fully scanned.
#
# If actual secrets are added to any of these files, they should use
# `${{ secrets.* }}` references (which don't embed the secret value) — never
# literal values.
